reply.body:
@Mikep86  What I mean is currently there are two overlapping tests: `testRerankInferenceResultCountMismatch` and `testRerankInvalidDocumentIndices`. Both set the `top_n` to be less than `rank_window_size`, the latter also corrupts some document indices in the reranker respose to point outside the returned range. This is what was throwing the IOOE the first place.

This state however will never be reached with this fix in place, because if `top_n < rank_window_size`, we fail fast without ever reaching the result processing and finding the invalid indices. So I don't see a point for keeping both test cases.

There _is_ a possible use case where `top_n == rank_window_size` and yet there is an `index` property pointing outside the N elements. But this means there's something broken in the reranker and it doesn't honor the API contract, which is not under our control. I'd argue whether we want to add new code to validate the result and throw an exception if the indices are off, or just let the IOOE cover it. (If we did that, where do we draw the line for validation? Checking that they are all integers? Ensuring there are no duplicates? etc.)

